 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue
+
+A queue belongs to **one** entity and has:
+
+- A `name` and a `slug` that is unique **within the entity**.
+- An `is_active` flag (default `true`). Inactive queues cannot issue new tickets.
+- Optional localized labels (`labels_i18n`, a JSON object keyed by locale).
+
 ### Ticket
 
-Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
+Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call**. After issuance every field is immutable except `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (the latter two are filled when called). Attributes:
 
 - Source queue — the ticket belongs to **one** queue (within the entity); does not record which totem it came from.
-- Priority (numeric, see below).
-- Issuance timestamp (ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
-- Issuance channel: totem, virtual, printed copy, etc.
+- Display `code`, unique per queue (see below).
+- Priority (integer `>= 0`, see below).
+- Issuance timestamp (`issued_at`; ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
+- Issuance channel: `totem`, `virtual` or `printed`.
+- Status: `waiting` or `called` (there is no cancelled status).
+- Call fields: `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (the attendant who called it).
+
+### Ticket code
+
+The display code is a per-queue sequence: `A001`, `A002`, … (prefix `A`, at least
+three digits, zero padded; values beyond 999 simply grow, e.g. `A1000`,
+`A1001`). The next code is the **last valid code + 1 by insertion order**,
+never by string order. Deleting a ticket does not make its number available to
+the next insertion while a later valid code exists. Malformed legacy codes
+(anything not matching `A<digits>`) are skipped when looking for the last valid
+code.
+
+Codes are unique per `(queue_id, code)`. Concurrent issuance retries a few
+times when two requests allocate the same code.
+
+### Issuing a ticket
+
+Issuance is rejected when the queue is inactive. The queue state is checked
+from the database inside the issuance transaction (with the queue row locked),
+so a queue deactivated after the caller loaded it is still rejected and nothing
+is persisted. New tickets are `waiting`, `issued_at = now()`, priority `0` and
+channel `virtual` unless another priority/channel is supplied. Negative
+priorities are rejected.
+
+### Calling a ticket
+
+A waiting ticket can be called **exactly once**. Calling marks it `called` and
+records `called_at` and `called_by_user_id`. Only a member of the ticket's
+entity may call it; anyone else receives an authorization error and the ticket
+is unchanged. Calling a ticket that is not waiting is a domain error. Selection
+of *which* waiting ticket to call next (priority/fairness) is a separate rule.
 
